media: h713-hdmirx: a source the firmware's table does not carry

A 1600x900 source on the HY310 reads "change in flight" and keeps reading it
for as long as it is plugged in (21.09.2026, dev19). Nothing is in flight:
the source is steady, the flip pointers move, INCAP names the geometry, and
the display firmware simply has no record for that raster - so it will never
lock it, and the driver has known that since 0136h without being able to say
it. QUERY_DV_TIMINGS answered -ENOLCK, whose documented meaning is "the
signal was unstable and the hardware could not lock on to it", and h713-tv
did the only sensible thing with that: it waited.

This patch gives that case its own answer, -ERANGE.

Why -ERANGE and not a new errno. QUERY_DV_TIMINGS documents four: ENODATA
(no DV timings on this input), ENOLINK (no signal), ENOLCK (unstable, could
not lock), ERANGE (timings were found, but they are out of range of the
hardware capabilities). Three are taken here, and the fourth already says
this exactly. What this receiver can lock IS the display firmware's mode
table: 0136h matches against it, and 0136k derives the capability cap from
its largest active records. A geometry in neither is out of the receiver's
range in the precise sense the documentation means, and it stays out of it
for as long as the source sends it - which is the difference to ENOLCK.
ENOENT or EPROTO would carry the distinction as well, but no generic V4L2
client has a word for either, and v4l2-ctl would print "Unknown error" where
ERANGE already prints the right sentence.

The timing is returned with the error. VIDIOC_QUERY_DV_TIMINGS carries
INFO_FL_ALWAYS_COPY, so the measurement reaches userspace together with the
-ERANGE, and h713-tv can name the source it is refusing instead of printing
a bare error.

Only with the table loaded. Without allwinner,firmware-tse the list is the
kernel's preset array, which has gaps a later poll may still close (576p50
is not in it at all), so a non-match there stays -ENOLCK as before. With the
table the list is complete and the answer is final. The pre-existing -ERANGE
of the capability check goes through the same path, because it is the same
statement about the same list.

One line per state, not per poll. The INCAP active word of the refused
source is remembered, and the line is printed only when it changes; it is
cleared again on a lock and on "no signal", so unplugging and replugging the
same source says it once more. The mid-day run's 158 lines in 30 seconds
came from a per-poll line (doku/128 section 3 item 1) and this does not
repeat that.

h713-tv, in the same commit: capture_signal() answers 3 for -ERANGE,
evaluate() stops retrying, hides the plane and logs "signal 1600x900p60,
108.0 MHz not in the firmware's table -- console" once per state change, and
`ctl status` and `-n` say the same. The retry budget is not spent on a
source that cannot lock.

Source: umbau/work/T2/REPORT.txt section 7.3, doku/128 section 3 item 2,
Documentation/userspace-api/media/v4l/vidioc-query-dv-timings.rst.
Follow-up to 0136h and 0136k; both stay byte-identical.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

diff -ruN a/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c b/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c
--- a/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c
@@ -1134,6 +1134,14 @@ struct h713_hdmirx {
 	u32 lock_total;
 	u32 lock_rowbyte;
 	bool lock_valid;
+
+	/*
+	 * The INCAP active word of the source the receiver last refused as
+	 * unlisted, so the line below is printed once per state and not once
+	 * per QUERY_DV_TIMINGS. Zero means "nothing refused". Written only in
+	 * detect_timings(), which the ioctl lock serialises.
+	 */
+	u32 unlisted_active;
 	struct mutex sig_lock;
 	void __iomem *sig_map;
 	phys_addr_t sig_map_base;
@@ -3336,6 +3344,40 @@ static void h713_hdmirx_ring_geometry(struct h713_hdmirx *rx,
 			     how);
 }
 
+/*
+ * A source the receiver will not lock, said once.
+ *
+ * Which errno: QUERY_DV_TIMINGS defines four, and three of them are already
+ * spoken for here -- ENODATA "no DV timings on this input", ENOLINK "no
+ * signal", ENOLCK "the signal was unstable and the hardware could not lock on
+ * to it". The fourth is ERANGE, "timings were found, but they are out of
+ * range of the hardware capabilities", and that is exactly this case: the
+ * timing was measured, and the list of what this receiver can lock is the
+ * display firmware's own mode table (0136h), from which the capability cap of
+ * 0136k is also derived. A geometry that is in neither is out of the
+ * receiver's range, and it will stay out of it for as long as the source
+ * sends it -- which is the difference to ENOLCK, and the reason 1600x900 read
+ * "change in flight" for ever until now (T2 7.3, doku/128 section 3 item 2).
+ *
+ * An errno outside the four (ENOENT, EPROTO) would carry the distinction too,
+ * but no generic client has a word for it. The measured timing is left in @t:
+ * QUERY_DV_TIMINGS is INFO_FL_ALWAYS_COPY, so userspace gets it with the
+ * error and can name the source it is refusing.
+ */
+static int h713_hdmirx_unlisted(struct h713_hdmirx *rx, u32 active,
+				unsigned int fps_x100, const char *why)
+{
+	if (rx->unlisted_active != active) {
+		rx->unlisted_active = active;
+		dev_info(rx->dev,
+			 "source %ux%u at %u.%02u Hz: %s -- no lock\n",
+			 active >> 16, active & 0xffff, fps_x100 / 100,
+			 fps_x100 % 100, why);
+	}
+
+	return -ERANGE;
+}
+
 /*
  * What the source is sending, from the three things the ARM can see:
  *
@@ -3387,6 +3429,7 @@ static int h713_hdmirx_detect_timings(struct h713_hdmirx *rx,
 		if (READ_ONCE(rx->rearm_busy))
 			return -ENOLCK;	/* a release is running, not a new source */
 		h713_hdmirx_forget_lock(rx);
+		rx->unlisted_active = 0;
 		return -ENOLINK;
 	}
 
@@ -3397,6 +3440,7 @@ static int h713_hdmirx_detect_timings(struct h713_hdmirx *rx,
 	if (have_sig && (sig.signal_id == H713_SIGNAL_ID_NONE ||
 			 !sig.h_size || !sig.v_size)) {
 		h713_hdmirx_forget_lock(rx);
+		rx->unlisted_active = 0;
 		return -ENOLINK;
 	}
 
@@ -3472,6 +3516,7 @@ static int h713_hdmirx_detect_timings(struct h713_hdmirx *rx,
 		h713_hdmirx_forget_lock(rx);
 	if ((!have_sig || own_record) && READ_ONCE(rx->lock_valid)) {
 		*t = rx->lock_timings;
+		rx->unlisted_active = 0;
 		return 0;
 	}
 
@@ -3501,11 +3546,24 @@ static int h713_hdmirx_detect_timings(struct h713_hdmirx *rx,
 	bt->vsync = vtotal - height;
 
 	if (!h713_hdmirx_match_preset(rx, t, htotal, vtotal, fps_x100, tolerance) &&
-	    !have_sig)
-		return -ENOLCK;
+	    !have_sig) {
+		/*
+		 * Without the firmware's table this is still "no preset agreed
+		 * with the measurement", which a later poll can resolve. With
+		 * it, the list is complete and the answer is final.
+		 */
+		if (!rx->tse_count)
+			return -ENOLCK;
+
+		return h713_hdmirx_unlisted(rx, active, fps_x100,
+					    "in none of the firmware's mode records");
+	}
 
 	if (!v4l2_valid_dv_timings(t, &h713_hdmirx_timings_cap, NULL, NULL))
-		return -ERANGE;
+		return h713_hdmirx_unlisted(rx, active, fps_x100,
+					    "beyond what the firmware's table locks");
+
+	rx->unlisted_active = 0;
 
 	/* have_sig is by now exactly "a genuine HDMI record" (own_record above) */
 	if (have_sig) {
